        <h1 class="heading-xl measure">After-hours answering and dispatch for NWA contractors</h1>
        <p class="body-lg measure" style="margin-top:1.15rem">
          The phone rings while the crew is on a roof, under a house, or halfway up I-49.
          The caller with no heat, a gas smell, an active leak, or storm damage is not
          waiting until morning. Arkansas Automated builds a 24/7 answering setup for
          Northwest Arkansas contractors that answers in your business's voice, sorts an
          emergency from a bookable job by rules you write, and either reaches your on-call
          tech or books the slot. $2,500 to set up, $497 a month flat, no per-minute charges.
        </p>
        <p class="measure" style="margin-top:1rem">
          The Fayetteville-Springdale-Rogers metro counted 622,177 residents in 2025 and ranked
          as the 9th fastest-growing U.S. metro
          (<a class="text-link" href="https://talkbusiness.net/2026/04/northwest-arkansas-ranks-9th-fastest-growing-u-s-metro-first-time-in-top-10-since-at-least-2020/" rel="noopener">Talk Business &amp; Politics, April 2026</a>).
          More households means more after-hours calls to the HVAC, plumbing, roofing, and
          electrical shops that cover the corridor. This page is the contractor dispatch
          version of that problem. The city page for every Fayetteville business is
          <a class="text-link" href="/answering-service-fayetteville-ar/">answering service for Fayetteville, AR</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="fine-print" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          Last updated September 2026. Written by Andre Brassfield, founder of Arkansas Automated.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <section class="section section-tint">
      <div class="container article-body">
        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">What counts as an emergency by trade</h2>
        <p>
          You write the line before the first after-hours call. The setup does not move a
          call across that line on its own. These four are the distinctions we will put in
          a script without you inventing a fifth. You can make a line stricter. We will not
          loosen one with a number, a code section, or a legal definition we made up.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>HVAC: no heat below 40°F.</strong> That is an emergency. No heat above that line is a booking, unless you write a different rule. We do not add a cooling-temperature cutoff on this page. Summer AC volume is a surge problem, not a second threshold.</li>
          <li><strong>Gas smell.</strong> Emergency, on whichever trade line the caller reached. The on-call tech you named for that condition gets the page. We do not add a ppm number, and we do not add an evacuate instruction you did not write.</li>
          <li><strong>Plumbing: an active leak.</strong> Water that is still moving and wetting floors, cabinets, or the space below is an emergency. A drip, a slow drain, or a toilet that is not overflowing books the next slot. If the caller has already shut the water off and nothing is still wetting anything, it stays a booking if that is the rule you wrote. The setup asks. It does not guess.</li>
          <li><strong>Roofing: storm damage.</strong> An opening, or water coming in from the storm, reaches the on-call tech. A quote for a roof that is not taking water books a look. It does not page someone at 11 p.m. We do not add a hail size or a wind speed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Electrical does not get a threshold from this page. A dead outlet, a flickering
          light, or a panel-upgrade quote is a booking unless you say otherwise. If you want
          a specific electrical condition to dispatch now, you write that condition, and we
          build it. A call that does not match a rule you approved does not get a guessed
          emergency.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the caller is a property manager sending you to a unit, their side of that
          handoff is on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/property-management-answering-service-northwest-arkansas/">property management answering page</a>.
          You still only roll to the address and the vendor list you already agreed to.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">How 60-second emergency dispatch to your on-call tech works</h2>
        <p>
          When a call matches an emergency rule you approved, the dispatch goes out within
          60 seconds. The handoff carries the caller's name, callback number, address, and
          the problem in the words the triage questions captured. It is not a "call the
          office" page with none of that. Sixty seconds is the page going out. It is not a
          promise the tech picked up, and it is not a measured average from our accounts.
        </p>
        <p>
          Who gets the page is a drive-time question, not one phone for the whole metro.
          You name the zones. A call in south Fayetteville and a call in Bentonville are
          not the same truck when your people are spread along I-49 and Highway 71. The
          setup uses the address against the zones you wrote. It does not send the
          Fayetteville tech north because they were next on a single list, unless that is
          the rule you wrote. We are not publishing a minute table for the corridor. We
          did not measure one for this page. You set the boundaries from the drives your
          techs already know.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some shops run one operation across Northwest Arkansas and the Fort Smith River
          Valley. That combined service area is a model already in this market. The zones
          have to include a River Valley address if you actually serve it. A tech covering
          the Fayetteville-Bentonville corridor is not automatically the tech for a River
          Valley call, and the setup will not assume they are.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the on-call tech does not accept, the backup you named is next. If nobody
          accepts, the manager you named gets the same facts. We do not keep a tech roster
          of our own, and we do not send a contractor you did not name. Every emergency
          call is recorded and transcribed, the same as every other call.
        </p>
        <p>
          A non-emergency does not use this path. If the first ring was missed because the
          crew was in the truck, that is a different problem, covered on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/missed-call-text-back-northwest-arkansas/">missed call text back page</a>.
          A text is not a substitute for the emergency page.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Booking non-emergency calls into ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, and Jobber</h2>
        <p>
          A call that is not an emergency should not become a voicemail you return in the
          morning. It gets a real slot on the calendar the shop already opens.
        </p>
        <p>
          ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, and Jobber are the three we book into when that is
          the system you run. Shops in this market already run them. The appointment, the
          address, the job type, and the notes the script captured land on that calendar,
          so the office is not retyping a transcript at 7 a.m. We do not ask you to switch
          tools to get after-hours booking, and we do not claim a certified native sync
          into any of the three. If a field the tool needs is not in the script, it does
          not get invented.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a job needs a look before a price, it books the look. The setup quotes only
          a range you put in the script, or nothing. It does not invent a ticket total
          on a call nobody has seen.
        </p>
        <p>
          The voice that answers is the
          <a class="text-link" href="/ai-receptionist/">AI receptionist</a>.
          The calendar layer, separate from the phone, is the
          <a class="text-link" href="/booking-software/">booking software</a>
          setup. Neither one replaces the office manager who owns the board in the morning.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Surviving NWA storm season with surge capacity</h2>
        <p>
          Three seasons hit the same phone. The rules do not change unless you wrote a
          seasonal rotation before the week arrived.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Spring tornadoes.</strong> Storm-damage calls already have an emergency
          path. The rest of the spring calls, "is my roof okay" questions and quote
          requests with no water coming in, book a look or take a message. They do not all
          page the on-call tech. If they do, the emergency list is useless by the second day.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Summer AC peaks.</strong> Northwest Arkansas has humid subtropical summers,
          and those summers drive AC-call spikes. An industry analysis cites Arkansas among
          the highest-demand states for HVAC AI answering
          (<a class="text-link" href="https://enumsol.com/ai-answering-for-hvac" rel="noopener">Enumsol, HVAC AI answering</a>).
          That is their analysis, not a count we measured on NWA tickets, and it is not a
          promise about your July. We do not add an AC temperature cutoff here. If you want
          no-cool above a line you choose to dispatch now, you write that line. Until you
          do, a cooling call books the next slot, and the spike is a volume problem, not a
          new emergency definition.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Winter ice.</strong> No heat below 40°F is already the emergency. Calls
          that are not that book the next slot. Ice changes how many of those calls arrive
          at once. It does not rewrite the line.
        </p>
        <p>
          Surge capacity is the roster, written before the week hits. You name who is on
          call, who is backup, and which stretch of I-49 and Highway 71 they cover. A 1-2
          truck shop and a multi-truck shop do not have the same bench. The setup does not
          invent extra techs. It uses the list you gave it, including the overflow path
          when the first tech is already on a job. If that path is a detailed message and
          a text to the owner, that is what the caller gets. We will not tell them a truck
          is rolling when it is not.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Bilingual crews and callers</h2>
        <p>
          Northwest Arkansas has an English and Spanish caller base, and the crews covering
          these jobs are bilingual too. A setup that only handles English is not answering
          for the calls you actually get. A dispatch that does not note the caller's
          language can send a tech who cannot finish the conversation on site.
        </p>
        <p>
          What our build can and cannot do in Spanish, today, is something we demonstrate
          on the assessment call rather than promise in print. Bring a real call type. You
          will hear it. If you want the page to say the caller was speaking Spanish, that
          is a question in the script you approve, and the tech you named for those calls
          is the one who gets it. We do not invent a translation of a diagnosis the script
          was not written to capture.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">Pricing tiers for 1-2 truck shops vs. multi-truck operations</h2>
        <p>
          One published price sheet, the same everywhere in Arkansas. Truck count does not
          get a second rate, and this page will not estimate one.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Setup:</strong> $2,500</li>
          <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> $497 flat, managed service</li>
          <li><strong>Included:</strong> 24/7 answering in your business's voice, emergency triage by your rules, dispatch to the on-call tech you named, booking on the calendar you already use (ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, or Jobber when that is the calendar), SMS follow-up, call recordings and transcripts, a monthly tuning session</li>
          <li><strong>Not charged:</strong> no per-minute charges and no per-call charges. The Front Door website fee ($497) credits toward this build if started within 60 days.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>1-2 truck shops.</strong> One on-call rotation, one backup, one set of
          zones. The owner is often the backup. The script stays short because the bench
          is short. When both techs are on jobs, the overflow path you wrote is the
          product. We do not pretend a two-truck shop has a third truck.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Multi-truck operations.</strong> More names on the rotation, and zones
          that matter: I-49 and Highway 71 between Fayetteville and Bentonville, plus the
          Fort Smith River Valley if that shop serves both from one operation. Separate
          calendars if you already split trades or crews in ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro,
          or Jobber. Same $497. The monthly tuning is where the roster and the zones get
          adjusted. There is no per-truck adder on the published sheet.
        </p>
        <p>
          Industry estimates of what one recovered call is worth by trade are on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/missed-call-text-back-northwest-arkansas/">missed call text back page</a>,
          labeled there as industry estimates from a vendor pipeline, not as our results
          and not as a quote for your next job. Use them only as a neighborhood for the
          monthly fee, then check your own tickets.
        </p>
        <p>
          The published price next to named answering vendors is on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/learn/ai-receptionist-cost/">AI receptionist cost page</a>,
          the full sheet is at <a class="text-link" href="/pricing.md">pricing.md</a>, and the
          service detail is on the
          <a class="text-link" href="/services/ai-front-desk">AI front desk page</a>.
          If your volume is low and you only need a human to take messages, say so on the
          assessment. We will tell you if you should not buy this.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Call to action:</strong> Book the free 15-minute assessment at
          <a class="text-link" href="https://book.arkansasautomated.com/book/discovery-call">book.arkansasautomated.com</a>
          or use the <a class="text-link" href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
          Email <a class="text-link" href="mailto:dre@arkansasautomated.com">dre@arkansasautomated.com</a>
          if you would rather write. You hear it answer as your shop before you pay anything.
        </p>

        <h2 class="heading-lg measure">FAQs</h2>
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="heading-md">{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
